{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Prevalence of major depression in people after stroke is 25% at the acute stage and 31% at 3 months poststroke 2 ; however, few individuals receive effective management for depressive symptoms post-stroke. 3 Unaddressed depressive symptoms can lead to devastating effects; not only do they stunt progress in therapy, but they can also negatively affect stroke outcomes, with increased morbidity, mortality, and poorer functional recovery. 4 Previous studies have been conducted to identify appropriate interventions for post-stroke depression; two common types are pharmacological interventions and non-pharmacological interventions (NPIs). 5-9 Three meta-analyses have been conducted to provide synthesized evidence on the effectiveness of pharmacological interventions for depressive symptoms following stroke, but results were mixed. 5,6,9 ... Moreover, it is recognized that the application of pharmacological interventions might be further limited due to adverse effects, long response times, potential medication adverse events, and low adherence rates. 8,10,11 ... Currently, an abundance of evidence supports the effectiveness of NPIs for depression in other clinical populations. 10,13 Three reviews have identified several NPIs for post-stroke depression and have qualitatively provided evidence. 8,11,14",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge on depression after stroke, existing evidence for pharmacological and non-pharmacological interventions, and points to uncertainties/mixed results (e.g., pharmacological meta-analyses mixed; qualitative rather than quantitative NPI reviews)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Therefore, to address this research gap, this study aimed to conduct a meta-analysis to synthesize and compare the available evidence regarding the effectiveness of various NPIs for treating depression in people following stroke.",
      "reason": "The importance/need for the review is explained as addressing a research gap (lack of quantitative demonstration of effectiveness across NPI types) and synthesizing/comparing available evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Three reviews have identified several NPIs for post-stroke depression and have qualitatively provided evidence. 8,11,14 In particular, the latest review indicated that therapies demonstrating a reduction in depressive symptoms include problem-solving therapy, acupuncture, repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation, music therapy, exercise, light therapy, motivational interviewing, and robotic-assisted therapy. 8 However, none of these reviews quantitatively demonstrated the effectiveness of different types of NPIs.",
      "reason": "The manuscript discusses prior reviews and explicitly explains why a new review is needed (existing reviews are qualitative and do not quantitatively demonstrate effectiveness of different NPI types)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence provided describes a proposed mechanism of action of the interventions."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence provided considers a logic model or hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes for complex interventions/delivery contexts."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 72
}

{
